WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.388
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:08.060
BRIAN SINGER: We're going
to get started, everybody.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.800
Thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.950
I hope you enjoyed the keynote--

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.280
a lot of great speakers there.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.620
And we have some excellent
content for you today.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.160
My name is Brian Singer.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.360
I am the head of Cloud
Commerce product.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.640
Cloud Commerce encompasses
our GCP marketplace product,

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.730
as well as Orbitera,
white-label marketplaces,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:30.010
and multi-Cloud billing.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.770
We also have Roger
Pilc with us today.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.410
Roger is the EVP
and Chief Innovation

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.712
Officer at Pitney Bowes.

00:00:36.712 --> 00:00:38.170
And he's going to
talk a little bit

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.990
about Pitney Bowes' digital
transformation and efforts

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:42.879
to build an ecosystem.

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:44.920
So I think one of the
exciting things about cloud

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.812
as I've seen it mature is that
the strategies around why we're

00:00:47.812 --> 00:00:50.020
using Cloud, and why it's
important to our companies,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.130
have really evolved.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:54.660
In the beginning, we were
moving to cloud to save money.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:55.990
We could pay by the drink.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.930
We could use services
only as we needed them.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.160
And it was a lot quicker to
provision a VM to get something

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.269
up and running.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:08.710
As we've gotten a little bit
smarter about how we use Cloud,

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:12.130
security has actually
become a driver.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.110
What Google is able to do with
security in its data centers,

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.230
and what we're able
to do with increasing

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.464
levels of automation,
is secure our workloads

00:01:19.464 --> 00:01:21.130
in the Cloud to a
level that we've never

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.870
been able to do before.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:25.540
And as we've solved
these problems,

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.290
and as we've been able to get
better economies of scale out

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:32.440
of Cloud, we've actually
started to unlock new products

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.350
and ecosystems.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.520
So Cloud and some of the
characteristics of Cloud

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.200
have actually
allowed us to create

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.180
products that could
not have existed

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.289
before cloud platforms evolved.

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:45.580
And that's really what we're
going to talk about today,

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.850
is how do we actually
monetize a product

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:50.167
that we've built in Cloud?

00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:52.750
So we'll look a little bit about
pricing strategies and things

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:53.270
like that.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.170
And then how do we build an
ecosystem around a platform?

00:01:56.170 --> 00:01:59.290
Because as we've seen, some
of the most valuable companies

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.030
and products that
are out there today

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.660
actually are products that
have enabled an ecosystem.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.800
So we think about building
a product for Cloud,

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.400
we have to answer
some key questions.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.342
One, how do we monetize it?

00:02:12.342 --> 00:02:14.050
What's the right way
to ask our customers

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.000
to pay for that service?

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.050
And every company out there,
and you'll hear from Roger,

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.670
Pitney Bowes started
as a shipping company.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.490
And you'll see, every
company is actually

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:28.060
evolving into more of a
traditional ISV software

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:28.900
provider.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.990
As our data is moving
to Cloud, and as more

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:35.410
of the services that we provide
to our customers are digital,

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:38.020
everyone needs to start
thinking about how do we become

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.810
a software vendor ourselves.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.920
And inherent to that is
understanding the billing

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.930
models that are
available to us in Cloud,

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.590
and which models are right for
us as we build these services.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.050
And this is important to
everybody in a company,

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.402
from the BD guy who's
going out and selling it

00:02:52.402 --> 00:02:54.610
to the developer who's
actually building the product.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.350
Because you actually
have to think

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.175
about the pricing
and the billing model

00:02:58.175 --> 00:03:00.550
as you build out the product,
and you'll see why in a few

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.846
slides.

00:03:02.846 --> 00:03:04.970
So in the Cloud, there's
a couple of billing models

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.090
that have become very common.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.840
And there's some good
reasons for that.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.620
First is a subscription model,
which everyone is probably

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:13.280
familiar with.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.980
We all have subscriptions
in our day-to-day lives.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.920
The key things to think about
when it comes to subscription

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.790
are that it's based on
a capped set of usage.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.530
So you set the threshold
for what a customer can

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.230
use, typically within a plan.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:30.410
And that's basically
the amount you're

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.980
going to charge them in any
given month for use of that.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.100
Subscription works great
for non-variable workloads,

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:39.230
where it's not
changing that much.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.570
If you think about
network usage,

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:45.060
for example, that could change a
lot over the course of a month.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.560
So it's not great to have a
subscription to a network,

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.720
to a certain amount
of bandwidth.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.700
But consumption, on the other
hand, usage-based pricing,

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.160
has become much more
common for cloud services.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.990
We use consumption pricing
for GCP, for virtual machines.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:03.230
We use consumption
pricing for storage.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.050
And this is based on the
actual usage of a product.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:07.730
And we think about
this as being best

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:11.240
for variable workloads,
when you don't necessarily

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.420
have a flat amount of
usage over many months

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.800
or in a given
month, and you want

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:17.966
to bill the customer for
exactly what they're using.

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:24.320
So looking a little bit more
closely at subscription,

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.590
it's that fixed monthly
price, usually prorated

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.060
to a given date.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.070
And there's a few
actions that are usually

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.510
available to a
customer when they

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:35.390
have a subscription, such
as upgrading or downgrading

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:36.830
between plans.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:38.450
There's actually a
lot of complexity

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.575
here that you need to think
about when building out

00:04:40.575 --> 00:04:44.060
your service because the
upgrade and the downgrade

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.700
could be manual You could
say to a customer we're

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.290
going to cut you off unless
you go upgrade your plan.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.890
It could also be automated
based on certain business

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.334
logic or rules that you
want to put in place.

00:04:54.334 --> 00:04:57.000
You could say we're going to let
you use up to a certain amount,

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.500
and if you go over
that, we're going

00:04:58.500 --> 00:04:59.850
to put you into another tier.

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:01.850
So when you think about
monetizing your service,

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.766
you have to make sure
you have a platform that

00:05:03.766 --> 00:05:07.552
can support whatever model
it is that you choose.

00:05:07.552 --> 00:05:10.010
The other thing that's very
interesting about subscriptions

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:12.200
is that you can have what
we call a "one-to-many

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.940
relationship" between
the entitlement,

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.240
which is the thing that the
customer is actually buying

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:23.520
from you, and the pricing
plan that they're using.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.440
What this means
is you could have,

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:29.330
for example, one plan that
is a yearly commitment that's

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:32.210
paid monthly, and you could
have another plan that

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.750
is a yearly commitment
that's paid yearly,

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:37.550
both for the same entitlement.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:39.350
In either case, the
customer is actually

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.150
going be paying a different
amount, most likely,

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.620
but they're paying
for the same thing.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.090
And this one-to-many
relationship

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.010
is really core to how
subscriptions work.

00:05:48.709 --> 00:05:50.250
So moving on to
consumption building,

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.470
there's a few different
types of consumption billing

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:56.430
that you could build into
a service that you have.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.870
On-demand is obviously
the most simple.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:02.670
It's just what I'm using
times the price for each item.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:06.960
In the case of GCP, there's
a price for a VM for an hour.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.690
You take the number
of hours of that VM

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.850
and multiply it by the
price, and that gives you

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.840
your total cost.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.070
And so the benefit of this
is it's very simple, easy

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.300
to understand.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.240
The drawback is that it's also
a little bit inflexible, which

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.400
is why we have what we
call "tiered pricing"

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.530
for consumption as well.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:30.400
And what this means is that
as you start to use more,

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.170
you pay less.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.840
And you see this more in
storage, and networking,

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.800
and things like that,
where you kind of

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:39.636
expect to derive some
economies of scale.

00:06:39.636 --> 00:06:41.010
And you can see
the example here,

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:47.370
where as we go from 20 widgets
to 30 widgets to 30 plus,

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:50.220
the actual price per
widget is coming down.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:55.920
And the cost, each tier
there gets lower and lower.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.390
The nice thing here is that
as the customer is using more,

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:01.684
they're getting the benefit
of that discount, which

00:07:01.684 --> 00:07:04.100
is actually going to keep them
consuming from you if there

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.505
are other alternatives.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:12.130
One of the most popular
models that we see out there,

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:14.770
though, is what we call a
"base plus consumption model."

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:17.380
This combines elements
of subscription billing

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.150
with consumption billing.

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:22.720
If you think about a
committed use discount in GCP,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.420
for example, there
is essentially

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:28.090
a subscription, a
commitment, and then there

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:32.980
is a per-widget fee or per-hour
fee if you go over that.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:34.930
The benefit of this
is that it allows

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:40.000
you to charge the customer for
the basic level of what they're

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:40.547
going to use.

00:07:40.547 --> 00:07:42.130
You can probably
forecast that they're

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:44.140
going to have some
basic level of usage.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:45.880
And you can lock that in.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.530
You can give them a
discount for that,

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:51.850
and then anything over that,
they're still able to consume,

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:53.986
but they're just going
to pay a little bit more.

00:07:53.986 --> 00:07:56.590
And this tends to be
the most flexible,

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.718
and actually confers a lot
of benefits to everybody.

00:08:03.279 --> 00:08:05.570
One of the things that you
need to think about, though,

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:07.319
if you are going to
do consumption billing

00:08:07.319 --> 00:08:10.130
is what are the metrics
you're going to bill against.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:12.350
Some of the common
metrics that we see

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:15.020
are time, so number
of hours, for example,

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:17.750
that somebody is using your
service, data, how much data

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:24.020
is being transferred, storage,
or just a count of something.

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:26.390
It could be the
number of connections

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.940
that the users made
to the service.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:32.030
The key is that this is
where the business really

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:34.280
has to come together
with the engineering team

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.440
to figure out what
are the right metrics,

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:38.750
and then how do we count
them, and how do we monetize

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:39.749
on them for our service?

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:45.030
So just putting it all
together real quickly,

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:48.480
whether you have a subscription
or a consumption plan,

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.070
you actually have
to figure out what

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.390
is the route to market,
then, for this product.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.810
So we have a few
different options.

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.120
You could sell it directly
to an end customer.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:00.540
You could also bring it to
market through a public Cloud

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.240
marketplace like
GCP marketplace.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.727
And you can also sell it
through your own channel.

00:09:05.727 --> 00:09:07.560
No matter what the
billing model is, though,

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.516
you have to make sure
that billing model fits

00:09:09.516 --> 00:09:10.890
with the go-to-market
that you're

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:11.973
choosing for your product.

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.770
So great-- you've created a
wonderful new product on Cloud.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:18.970
You've added monetization.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:20.850
You've picked your
route to market.

00:09:20.850 --> 00:09:22.600
Now comes the fun part,
where you actually

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:26.590
get to go build an ecosystem
around this product.

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:29.800
I think it's important to
note that an ecosystem is not

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:30.640
an app store.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.470
And just because you
have an app store,

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.350
it doesn't mean you
have an ecosystem.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:38.745
I actually found this
definition in a paper

00:09:38.745 --> 00:09:40.120
as I was preparing
for this talk.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:42.340
And I really thought it
did a good job of outlining

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:44.632
what an ecosystem actually is.

00:09:44.632 --> 00:09:46.090
So I'm going to
read it real quick.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:50.650
"A software ecosystem is a set
of actors functioning as a unit

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:53.020
and interacting with a
shared market for software

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:54.970
and services.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:57.190
These relationships
are frequently

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:00.700
underpinned by a common
technological platform

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:04.330
or market and operate through
the exchange of information,

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.730
resources, and artifacts."

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.430
So the really
interesting thing to me

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:13.330
is that all ecosystems have this
common technological platform

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:14.722
in common.

00:10:14.722 --> 00:10:16.180
And this is really
core to actually

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:17.740
being able to build
out an ecosystem.

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.040
So if you think about what has
to be in place before you can

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.610
even think about creating an
ecosystem around your product,

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.020
you have to have this
common tech platform.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.000
It also really helps to
have a stable customer base.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.760
If you don't have customers
that are using your product,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.190
it's very hard to
attract other companies

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:41.710
to build around the platform
that you've created.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:44.760
So it's a question that
we get a lot of the time

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:47.640
is, should I work on building
the ecosystem first or building

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.260
the customer base first?

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:51.109
I would say that building
the customer base

00:10:51.109 --> 00:10:52.650
is very, very
important, because it's

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.775
hard to attract that
ecosystem if you don't have it

00:10:54.775 --> 00:10:55.860
in the first place.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:58.950
Your customers also need to
be engaged with your platform.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.020
An ecosystem will
only really form

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.700
if they believe that
they can make money

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.810
by working with you and
with your customers.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.060
And if the customers aren't
engaged with the platform,

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.120
it's hard to actually get
that flywheel spinning.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.060
And then finally, you have to
be able to create a trusted

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.550
relationship with
your customers.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.560
What you're doing effectively
by creating an ecosystem,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:22.320
and maybe wrapping an
app store around it,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:25.380
is saying that you
actually believe

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:30.690
these solutions are good.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:32.760
Your customers are going
to be able to use them.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.690
They're going to work,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:36.648
And if you don't have
that trusted relationship

00:11:36.648 --> 00:11:38.400
with your customers,
it's very difficult

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:42.190
to get them to start using
third-party software.

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:44.440
In terms of the actual
product requirements,

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:46.310
a couple of things
have to be in place.

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:48.520
First of all, you have
to be able to support

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:50.590
the exchange of information.

00:11:50.590 --> 00:11:52.690
In today's world,
that usually means

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:56.810
that your platform has open
APIs that third parties can use.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:01.300
So if you have data that's
part of your platform,

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.430
that you're making
available to your customers,

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:05.950
you want other third parties
to be able to provide services

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:06.970
on top of that.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:09.700
You have to have some way
for them to get at that data.

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.820
So open APIs, extensibility
are very important in creating

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:15.620
an ecosystem.

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.680
A couple of great examples--

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.960
everyone's probably
familiar with Google Play.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.960
I think, actually Sundar talked
about the Android ecosystem

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:23.570
a little bit.

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:26.140
It's more than just,
obviously, the app store.

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:31.570
It's all the telcos, all the
customers, all the app makers.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:33.910
And all of these entities
are working together

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.190
to deliver value
to the end user.

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:37.840
The common tech
platform in this case

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:40.821
is, obviously, the
Android operating system.

00:12:40.821 --> 00:12:42.820
And you can kind of see
how some of the elements

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.220
in that definition
apply to Android.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:47.620
GCP is another example
of an ecosystem.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:48.520
We're all here.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:50.812
We're all part of
that ecosystem.

00:12:50.812 --> 00:12:52.270
And some of the
things we're trying

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:54.730
to do to help that
ecosystem organize and form

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:59.376
are things like GCP marketplace,
which we've made available.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:04.430
So just a quick bit--

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:05.990
why are ecosystems important?

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:08.100
I think some of this
goes without saying,

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:11.330
but for one, it increases
loyalty and stickiness

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:13.640
from the customers.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:15.830
If they're getting
products, not just from you

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:18.860
but from other entities, and
you're facilitating that,

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:21.350
they're going to stick with
you over the long term.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:24.710
Second, it helps fill gaps
in the platform offering.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:27.350
It's very difficult to
have a solution that

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:29.330
solves all the problems
that your customers

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:30.770
want you to solve.

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:32.690
You will not be able to
hire enough engineers

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:33.900
to make that happen.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.690
But if you have a
robust ecosystem,

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:37.700
they can fill in these gaps.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:39.590
And then finally,
it gives you avenues

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:42.600
for additional monetization
and value creation.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.080
So it goes without saying,
if you build a marketplace,

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.538
people are going to be buying
things from that marketplace.

00:13:47.538 --> 00:13:50.840
And whoever is facilitating
that transaction

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:55.174
is able to take a
little bit of that value

00:13:55.174 --> 00:13:56.090
that they're creating.

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:01.490
So the ecosystems
can actually form

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:03.020
without you doing anything.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:05.930
You'll see, if you have a great
product and customers come

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:07.790
into that product,
other third parties

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:09.260
are going to want
to get engaged.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:12.680
But to really organize
and monetize effectively,

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:17.210
it helps to actually have
a place for customers

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:23.280
to go to discover solutions,
and procure them, and so on.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.120
So in terms of actually
operationalizing,

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:27.590
we look at it from two sides--

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.480
the partner side, so
these are the entities

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:34.020
that are creating value-added
solutions around your product.

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.680
And in the case of them,
there's really three things

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:37.550
to think about--

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:40.370
onboarding, and this
means bringing them

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:45.320
into your ecosystem,
getting the contract signed,

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:48.950
doing sort of the actual
product integration

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:53.690
work, certification, so that
means looking at the solutions

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:57.890
that you're bringing
into the marketplace,

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.470
and actually running
them through some sort

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:03.199
of certification process so that
when your customers are using

00:15:03.199 --> 00:15:05.240
them, they can be relatively
assured that they're

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.420
going to work, and they can
trust the solutions that are

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.550
available from your partners.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.950
And then finally, go to market.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:16.250
It's not enough to just usually
put solutions out there.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.770
Your partners, whoever
is participating

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:21.370
in your ecosystem, is
going to need help selling.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.320
On the customer side,
there are some basic things

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:29.410
that customers need in order to
make the ecosystem effective.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:32.020
They have to be able to find the
solutions that are out there.

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:34.920
This is where the app store
becomes very important,

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:37.940
app store marketplace,
whatever you want to call it.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:39.844
They have to be able
to purchase solutions.

00:15:39.844 --> 00:15:41.260
And it really helps
for them to be

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:43.900
able to purchase from
a central entity.

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:45.940
You don't want your
customers to have

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:49.720
to go to many
different entities,

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:52.360
many different organizations
to use products along

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:53.830
with your platform.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:57.020
You want them to be able to
centralize that procurement.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:00.002
And then finally, provisioning--
that is basically,

00:16:00.002 --> 00:16:02.210
how are they going to use
these third-party products?

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:04.370
Provisioning can mean a
lot of different things.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:08.300
It could mean deploying
a VM to a cloud platform.

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.002
It could just mean
turning on an API.

00:16:11.002 --> 00:16:13.210
All three of these things
need to work really, really

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:18.130
easily for your customers
to have a robust ecosystem.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:21.940
So at Orbitera, we've
actually built a solution

00:16:21.940 --> 00:16:23.970
to help you jumpstart
your efforts with this.

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:25.720
We can't solve every
problem that you

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:27.559
have for building an
ecosystem, but some

00:16:27.559 --> 00:16:29.350
of the things we just
talked about in terms

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:32.290
of onboarding partners, and
actually making your solutions

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:37.210
discoverable, and making it
easy to integrate and provision,

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:39.630
are things that the Orbitera
White-label marketplace is

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:40.380
able to help with.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:43.990
And we'll hear from Roger
Pilc in just a few minutes

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:47.890
about how Pitney Bowes is using
Orbitera to build an ecosystem

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:49.460
marketplace for their customers.

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:54.710
Actually, perfect timing.

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.460
So I'm going to welcome
Roger up on stage.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:58.800
Roger is the Executive
Vice President and CEO

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:00.110
at Pitney Bowes.

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:02.119
Take it away, Roger.

00:17:02.119 --> 00:17:03.702
ROGER PILC: You said
a couple minutes.

00:17:03.702 --> 00:17:06.230
I thought I had a couple--

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:07.339
all right, great.

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:09.319
Good to be here.

00:17:09.319 --> 00:17:11.420
I'm going to share
Pitney Bowes' journey.

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:13.790
I'm sure part of it is
unique to our company,

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:15.869
but hopefully, some
of it is helpful

00:17:15.869 --> 00:17:17.930
and sounds familiar
to what some of you

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:20.960
are going through in
your own companies.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:24.329
Pitney Bowes is a
100-year-old company.

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:29.390
We have had a long history in
office equipment, and services

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:33.260
around mailing and shipping,
as Brian went through.

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:35.780
And our general
set of solutions,

00:17:35.780 --> 00:17:38.110
ultimately, power commerce.

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:41.420
100 years into it,
we had a new CEO

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:43.730
come on board six years ago.

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:45.680
We've undertaken
a pretty dramatic

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.810
digital transformation.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:49.850
Part of the
transformation relates

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:52.940
to a couple of billion
dollars of acquisitions

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:56.070
in the software space
that started 10 years ago.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:59.010
So in addition to the mailing
and shipping heritage,

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:01.050
we're in three
categories of software.

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:03.500
One is customer
information management.

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:07.060
We do data quality for
customers like Salesforce.com.

00:18:07.060 --> 00:18:10.190
We're in location intelligence--
we sell data to customers like

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:11.810
Twitter and Facebook--

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:13.410
and customer engagement.

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:16.980
So as we went through our
digital transformation,

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:20.390
it was a huge help that we
had 800 software developers

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:22.140
and a software business.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:25.590
We partner closely with GE, who
didn't necessarily have that.

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.800
And it's been a key part
of our transformation

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:29.266
of mailing and shipping.

00:18:29.266 --> 00:18:30.890
And then the last
piece of the equation

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:35.510
has been really important, and
that's e-commerce services.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:39.590
We do all of eBay's
global shipping platform,

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:43.880
and have grown our
global shipping

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.400
business and our domestic
e-commerce business

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:47.800
to a billion dollars now.

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:51.030
So between that and software,
it's half of our company.

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:55.340
And we got our first taste of
creating an ecosystem based

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:57.650
on a platform in that
e-commerce business.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:00.350
And that, ultimately, led
to partnering with Google

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:01.820
for our SMB business.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:04.100
But in our e-commerce
business, we're

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:10.250
actually both supporting
eBay and also 250 retailers.

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:14.540
We're connecting millions of
sellers, millions of buyers,

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:17.120
hundreds of retailers,
tens of payments companies,

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.660
tens of shippers via the
Pitney Bowes Commerce Cloud.

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:23.420
So that ended up
facilitating from zero

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:26.960
to almost $2 billion dollars
of gross merchandise value

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.864
by creating that platform
and that ecosystem

00:19:29.864 --> 00:19:30.780
in our newer business.

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:32.960
So based on that,
we said everything

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:36.800
we learned, let's take that
to a very traditional business

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:39.210
of ours, which was
what we were known for,

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:41.810
which is postage meters and
a million small businesses

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:43.220
using postage meters.

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:45.560
So it's a little
bit of our history.

00:19:45.560 --> 00:19:48.170
The journey has involved
a tremendous amount

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:49.910
of technology change.

00:19:49.910 --> 00:19:52.850
And that actually was
very, very critical

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:55.680
to the building of an ecosystem.

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:58.820
Brian referred a couple
of times to a platform,

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:01.970
and I think a key part of
the journey of building

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:06.710
this ecosystem has been a
very persistent building

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:08.800
of a platform over
multiple years.

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.360
Our CEO was very forward
looking, kind of not what I've

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:13.640
been used to, to be honest.

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:17.240
But he really encouraged
us to follow a journey.

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:20.330
We basically laid out a
set of technology drivers

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:23.510
like you'll see at events like
this, built the Commerce Cloud.

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:27.260
And there were three pillars
of our transformation.

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:29.216
One was design.

00:20:29.216 --> 00:20:30.590
So many of you at
your companies,

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:32.150
I'm sure you have
design initiatives.

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:36.440
But design and client experience
was very, very critical.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:38.610
For those million
small businesses,

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:41.300
we actually built
a client portal.

00:20:41.300 --> 00:20:45.030
So we have 4 million
logins now a quarter.

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:47.690
And that was very
critical as we started

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:50.900
to build this ecosystem
and this marketplace.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:54.080
So design and client experience
and creating a web experience

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:56.900
was the first piece of it.

00:20:56.900 --> 00:21:00.600
The second piece of it was
data and a data platform.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:03.710
So as we were progressing
on this journey,

00:21:03.710 --> 00:21:08.000
we built one very large data
platform, big-data platform,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.000
that served all the
company's data needs.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.350
It became our IoT platform.

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:14.780
It became our BI platform.

00:21:14.780 --> 00:21:17.430
It became our tracking platform.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:20.630
So we've got 30 products
across the company leveraging

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:22.940
our big-data platform.

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:26.240
And it's also the
place where we create

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:27.620
data about our customers.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:29.780
And that, also, will
feed into the app

00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:30.990
store and the marketplace.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:36.380
So as we build IoT capabilities,
we built web capabilities,

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.180
we started to build
increasing amounts

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:40.940
of data about our
million SMB customers.

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:45.410
And that's actually going to be
very critical to our app store

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:46.580
ecosystem creation.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:49.340
So that was a very critical
piece of the puzzle.

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:54.800
And then lastly, in terms of
foundations of our platform,

00:21:54.800 --> 00:21:59.030
we've basically transitioned
our entire 100-year-old company

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:01.190
to the Cloud, just went all in.

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:04.080
So we've got 35 SaaS products.

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:08.400
Those SMBs, those million
SMBs, almost 200,000

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:12.430
now have a SaaS or IoT
product of some sort.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:15.810
So we used to be an
office equipment company.

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:19.110
We sort of shifted to
a cloud-centric client

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:24.150
experience, and then started
offering our own IoT or SaaS

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:25.560
products to those customers.

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.150
And we sort of
changed the center

00:22:27.150 --> 00:22:29.610
of gravity of the
company from sort

00:22:29.610 --> 00:22:32.130
of office equipment
and a phone experience

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:34.080
to a Cloud and
SaaS-based experience

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:35.360
based on the Commerce Cloud.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.520
So SaaS was critical to it.

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:39.990
API was critical to it.

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:44.910
Brian mentioned open
platforms and APIs.

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.640
So a big reason why we're
here working with Orbitera

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:51.060
is several years ago we
jumped in with Apigee,

00:22:51.060 --> 00:22:55.170
which is the API management
platform that Google bought.

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:58.500
And we built our
whole API platform

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:01.740
based on Apigee,
now Google Apigee.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:04.020
We've got 400 APIs on that.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:08.280
A business that was
nothing grew to 120 million

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:10.680
annualized based on
that API platform.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:14.100
And so as we've built this
platform and ecosystem,

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:18.360
we're able to launch hackathons
and developer programs.

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:20.940
And they're all leveraging
all of our solutions

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.760
via that Google
Apigee API platform.

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:26.550
And then lastly, we
jumped in with mobile.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:29.760
And that's another reason
why we partnered with Google.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:33.490
The Apigee experience
was really, really good.

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:37.290
And we ultimately changed
that office equipment

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:42.140
to be Android based, thinking
we want to build ecosystems,

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:45.180
but we're also riding other's
ecosystems and platforms.

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:47.250
So we went all in.

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.530
And our latest generation
of office equipment, we

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:53.100
launched at Google's New York
headquarters last September.

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:54.000
It's Android based.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:57.130
It is the best selling new
product launch we ever had.

00:23:57.130 --> 00:24:00.990
We've already sold
over 60,000 of them.

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:05.190
And we've actually become
a relatively mature,

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:08.580
mobile-centric
IoT-based company.

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:11.430
We partnered with the
gentleman that created the Nest

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:13.990
thermostat, now part of Google.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.710
And so those devices, we
kind of transformed them

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:18.420
like Nest transformed
thermostats.

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:22.860
So now they're digital, IoT
connected to our Commerce Cloud

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:24.211
based on Google.

00:24:24.211 --> 00:24:25.460
So that's sort of our journey.

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:27.870
It's a little bit of context
for the conversation.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:29.730
Ultimately, it's about
business outcomes.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:32.285
I was e-mailing with my
boss, our CEO, on the flight.

00:24:32.285 --> 00:24:34.410
And he just pretty much
